---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { meta, software, HISTORY_MONTHS } from '../lib/data';

const scored = software.filter((s) => s.score !== null).map((s) => s.score!);
const dist = [
  { label: 'Thriving', range: '82–100', n: scored.filter((s) => s >= 82).length },
  { label: 'Healthy', range: '68–81', n: scored.filter((s) => s >= 68 && s < 82).length },
  { label: 'Steady', range: '52–67', n: scored.filter((s) => s >= 52 && s < 68).length },
  { label: 'Slowing', range: '36–51', n: scored.filter((s) => s >= 36 && s < 52).length },
  { label: 'At risk', range: '0–35', n: scored.filter((s) => s < 36).length },
];
const median = scored.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b)[Math.floor(scored.length / 2)];
---

<Base
  title="How the health score is calculated"
  description="The full method behind our 0–100 maintenance health score for self-hosted software: inputs, weights, thresholds and known limitations."
  path="/methodology"
>
  <div class="wrap narrow prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Methodology' }]} />
    <h1>How the health score is calculated</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:.7rem">
      The score answers one question: <em>is this project likely to still be maintained in two
      years?</em> It says nothing about features, security or fitness for your use case.
    </p>

    <h2>Inputs</h2>
    <p>
      Everything comes from public repository metadata in the
      <a href="https://github.com/awesome-selfhosted/awesome-selfhosted-data" rel="noopener">awesome-selfhosted</a>
      dataset: monthly commit counts, tagged releases, star counts and archive status. We add no
      private data and run no crawlers of our own. The snapshot behind this build is from
      <strong>{meta.dataDate.slice(0, 10)}</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The source keeps a rolling window of commit history whose most recent month is still in
      progress. We drop that partial month and measure the {HISTORY_MONTHS} complete months behind
      it, rather than assuming a fixed year — otherwise the oldest bar on every activity chart
      would read zero simply because the data does not go back that far.
    </p>

    <h2>The four components</h2>
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Points</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Maintenance</th><td class="num">40</td><td>Commits over the last {HISTORY_MONTHS} complete months, expressed as a percentile against every scored project</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Releases</th><td class="num">25</td><td>Days since the most recent tagged release. Absolute thresholds, not relative</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Community</th><td class="num">20</td><td>Repository stars, as a percentile against every scored project</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Momentum</th><td class="num">15</td><td>Commits in the last 3 months against the 3 months before</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Why percentiles</h2>
    <p>
      Two components are scored <em>relative to the whole catalogue</em> rather than against fixed
      thresholds. Absolute thresholds sound more objective but behave badly: pick any cutoff for
      "enough commits" and a large share of projects cluster at the ceiling, which means the score
      stops distinguishing anything. Ranking against {scored.length} peers guarantees the scale
      stays spread out, and it cannot be inflated by pushing trivial commits, because everyone
      else's rank moves too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Release recency and momentum stay absolute, because those two <em>do</em> have a meaning
      independent of the field. A release three years old is stale whether or not other projects
      are worse.
    </p>

    <h2>Current distribution</h2>
    <p>Across {scored.length} scored projects, the median is <strong>{median}</strong>.</p>
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Band</th><th>Score</th><th>Projects</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {dist.map((d) => (
          <tr>
            <th>{d.label}</th><td class="num">{d.range}</td><td class="num">{d.n}</td>
            <td class="num">{((100 * d.n) / scored.length).toFixed(1)}%</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What the score does not measure</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Security.</strong> An actively developed project can still be badly written. We measure activity, not code quality or audit history.</li>
      <li><strong>Features.</strong> A lower-scoring project may do exactly what you need while the leader does not.</li>
      <li><strong>Stability of small projects.</strong> A finished, single-purpose tool that needs no changes will score low on commit volume. That is a real limitation of the method, and why "at risk" is worded as a prompt to check rather than a verdict.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-GitHub development.</strong> Projects whose main activity happens on a mailing list, Codeberg or a private mirror will be understated.</li>
      <li><strong>Forks and successors.</strong> A project can be effectively continued under a new name; the score follows repositories, not lineages.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Projects with no score</h2>
    <p>
      Where there is no public repository to measure, we show <em>Unrated</em> rather than
      inventing a number. Archived repositories are shown as <em>Discontinued</em> and are excluded
      from the percentile base, so that dead projects do not drag the scale down and flatter
      everyone else.
    </p>

    <h2>Update cadence</h2>
    <p>
      The source dataset is refreshed weekly and the whole site is rebuilt from scratch each time,
      so every score, ranking and comparison on this site reflects the most recent snapshot.
      Nothing here is hand-written per project, which means nothing here goes quietly stale.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a project is misrepresented, the underlying data is community-maintained and open to
      correction — issues can be filed against the
      <a href="https://github.com/awesome-selfhosted/awesome-selfhosted-data" rel="noopener">upstream dataset</a>,
      and the fix will flow into the next weekly rebuild here. See also
      <a href="/attribution">data and attribution</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
